.TH ROTCHECK 1 "2026-10-28" "rotcheck 1.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rotcheck \- detect missing, changed and silently corrupted files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rotcheck hash
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B rotcheck list
.RI [ DIR ]
.br
.B rotcheck init
.RI [ DIR ]
.br
.B rotcheck verify
.RB [ \-\-strict ]
.RB [ \-\-quiet ]
.RB [ \-\-fast ]
.RI [ DIR ]
.br
.B rotcheck update
.RB [ \-\-accept\-corrupted ]
.RB [ \-\-quiet ]
.RI [ DIR ]
.br
.B rotcheck help
.br
.B rotcheck \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rotcheck
records a SHA\-256 checksum, size and modification time for every file in a
folder, and later tells you which files went missing, were edited, or changed
content without their size or timestamp changing (bit rot).
.PP
The record is a plain text file,
.IR DIR /.rotcheck.sha256 ,
in the format of
.BR sha256sum (1),
so
.B sha256sum \-c
can check the folder too. Sizes and times are kept on
.B #
comment lines that
.B sha256sum
skips. The format is described in
.I /usr/share/doc/rotcheck/FORMAT.md.
.PP
.I DIR
defaults to the current directory. Symbolic links are skipped, not followed.
rotcheck works offline and never changes your files.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI hash " FILE" ...
Print SHA\-256 checksums in
.B sha256sum
format.
.TP
.BR list " [\fIDIR\fR]"
List the files a manifest of
.I DIR
would cover: relative paths with
.BR / ,
sorted.
.TP
.BR init " [\fIDIR\fR]"
Record every file in
.IR DIR .
Refuses to overwrite an existing manifest.
.TP
.BR verify " [\fIDIR\fR]"
Hash every file again and compare with the manifest. Files that are fine
are not listed. Changes nothing.
.TP
.BR update " [\fIDIR\fR]"
Like
.BR verify ,
then rewrite the manifest: add new files, drop missing ones and record the
new checksum of edited ones. Files that look corrupted keep their old
checksum.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-strict
.RB ( verify )
Also exit with status 1 when there are new files.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
.RB ( verify ", " update )
Print only the files that make the command fail, and no summary. No output
means all is well, which suits
.BR cron (8).
.TP
.B \-\-fast
.RB ( verify )
Compare only size and modification time, without reading contents.
Differences are reported as
.BR MODIFIED .
This cannot detect bit rot. Entries without a recorded size and time are
hashed anyway.
.TP
.B \-\-accept\-corrupted
.RB ( update )
Also re\-record files that look corrupted.
.SH OUTPUT
.B verify
and
.B update
print one line per file: a status word, spaces up to column 13, then the
path. Names containing a backslash, newline or carriage return are escaped as
.B sha256sum
does. The summary line and errors start with
.B rotcheck:
and errors go to standard error.
.TP 12
.B CORRUPTED
Content changed but size and time did not. Likely bit rot; restore the file
from a backup.
.TP
.B MODIFIED
Content changed along with size or time. Probably an edit.
.TP
.B MISSING
In the manifest but no longer in the folder.
.TP
.B NEW
In the folder but not in the manifest.
.TP
.B UNREADABLE
Present but could not be read.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR DIR /.rotcheck.sha256
The manifest.
.TP
.IR DIR /.rotcheckignore
Files and folders to leave out, one pattern per line.
.B *
matches anything except
.BR / .
A pattern without
.B /
matches a name anywhere; one with
.B /
matches the full path from
.IR DIR .
A trailing
.B /
matches folders only. Lines starting with
.B #
and blank lines are skipped.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything recorded is intact (new files are ignored unless
.BR \-\-strict ).
For
.BR update ,
the manifest now matches the folder.
.TP
.B 1
Something is corrupted, modified, missing or unreadable. For
.BR update ,
a corrupted or unreadable file was left as it was.
.TP
.B 2
The check could not be done: bad arguments, no manifest, unreadable folder.
.SH EXAMPLES
Record a folder, then check it every Sunday from
.BR crontab (5):
.PP
.nf
.RS
rotcheck init ~/Pictures
0 3 * * 0  rotcheck verify \-\-quiet ~/Pictures
.RE
.fi
.PP
List only the corrupted files:
.PP
.nf
.RS
rotcheck verify \-\-quiet ~/Pictures | grep \(aq^CORRUPTED\(aq | cut \-c13\-
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sha256sum (1)
.PP
https://github.com/majidninja/rotcheck
